Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Convert markdown to HTML using a regex pattern
    std::regex markdownRegex(R"(^#(\w+)\s+(.*?)$");
    std::string html;
    std::smatch match;

    while (std::regex_search(markdown, match, markdownRegex)) {
        std::string title = match[1].str();
        std::string content = match[2].str();
        html += "<h1>" + title + "</h1><p>" + content + "</p>\n";
    }

    return html;
}
```

This function uses a regex pattern to match markdown headings and convert them to HTML. The regex pattern `^#(\w+)\s+(.*?)$` matches lines that start with a `#` character, followed by one or more word characters (which represent the heading level), a space, and then the rest of the line (which represents the heading content). The function then constructs an HTML string by wrapping the heading title in an `<h1>` tag and the heading content in a `<p>` tag, and appends the resulting HTML string to the `html` string before returning.

